The problem of targeted interactive search, where a user attempts to find one of multiple multiple hidden items by asking a budget-constrained question to a knowledgeable helper, is studied under new limits. Initially, the difficulty of this issue under a certain constraint has been proven to be NP-hard to determine its solution space, and further research has established a lower bound on the minimum number of interactions required to find one of the items with high confidence, which closely approaches optimal results in the best case scenarios. Moreover, the research has revealed the influence of the target distribution and the number of hidden items on the search complexity and offered strategies to enhance interaction efficiency in the presence of prior knowledge. Finally, a comprehensive analysis of the targeted interactive search problem under multiple targets has been presented, and the research has provided essential insights into how to balance the budget and the precision of the search results in the targeted interactive search setting. This study is an extension of our earlier work on targeted interactive search, and it is available at https://arxiv.org/submit/2109.11121/. A comprehensive implementation of our algorithms can be found at https://mohsenms.github.io/TISIS/. 	extbf{Copyright 	extcircled{R} 2023. All rights reserved.} 
